## Swift style (Apple clients)

Human-review baseline is the [Kodeco Swift style guide](https://github.com/kodecocodes/swift-style-guide) (naming per the Swift API Design Guidelines, 2-space indent, no semicolons, omit redundant `self`, golden-path `guard`, comments explain why). It is a baseline, not law — the deviations below win.

**Machine truth lives in the repo and CI enforces it:**

- Formatting: `apps/apple/.swiftformat` (`swiftformat .` to apply, `swiftformat --lint .` to check)
- Lint: `apps/apple/.swiftlint.yml` (`swiftlint lint --strict`)
- Both run from `apps/apple/`; both run as a blocking CI job on ready-for-review PRs touching `apps/apple/**`

**Deliberate deviations from the Kodeco guide:**

1. The guide is Swift-5-era and written for book/tutorial sample code. Swift 6 wins: strict concurrency (`Sendable`, checked `throws`), the `Testing` framework (`@Test`, `#expect`), and OpenAPI-generator call patterns (`.init(...)` inputs, `Components.Schemas.*`) stay as generated/idiomatic even where the guide is silent.
2. `line_length` is disabled in `.swiftlint.yml`. Package tests carry long inline Core JSON fixtures; wrapping them buys nothing and SwiftFormat does not wrap.
3. Tutorial-sample sections do not apply to this production app: copyright headers, organization/bundle-identifier boilerplate, print-oriented rules.
4. Do not add an Xcode Run Script build phase for lint (the Kodeco policy targets downloadable book samples). CI is the enforcement point; local runs are manual. Do not copy their `com.raywenderlich.swiftlint.yml` into this repo — ours is tuned to this tree.
